\ Walk along the gate positions from Foundation, gate zero.
\ G(n)=G(n-1)+positive interval(n) for positive n.
\ G(-n)=G(-(n-1))-negative interval(n) for positive n.
\ Each interval comes from a complete separate sauce calculation.
\ Very large gate indices require a future arbitrary-precision day axis.

A gate walk has
  a number called requested index,
  a number called current index,
  a calendar day called current position.

The gate walk is a gate walk.

\ Budget full conversion by number of exact gate gaps requested.
\ Every gap requires a complete separate sauce. A distant target must
\ never launch an effectively endless calculation from Foundation.
\ The cap is an implementation resource limit, not a calendar rule.
\ Only a new user-level year search or stand-alone gate lookup resets
\ the budget. All later year/cutlet walking shares the same allowance.
A gate step budget has
  a number called enabled,
  a number called steps remaining,
  a number called exhausted.

The gate step budget is a gate step budget.

To begin bounded gate walking:
  Put 1 into the gate step budget's enabled.
  Put 1024 into the gate step budget's steps remaining.
  Put 0 into the gate step budget's exhausted.

To request another bounded gate step:
  If the gate step budget's enabled is not 1, exit.
  If the gate step budget's steps remaining is less than 1,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Subtract 1 from the gate step budget's steps remaining.

\ Move one step towards a positive gate index.
\ Prepare both integer fields first; publish neither until the full
\ sauce returns one canonical interval from 42 through 963 days.
To move to the next positive gate:
  Request another bounded gate step.
  If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
  If the gate walk's current index is greater than or equal to 400000000,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Put the gate walk's current index into a prospective positive index number.
  Add 1 to the prospective positive index number.
  Put the prospective positive index number into a positive gap index number.
  If the gate walk's current index is less than 0,
    put the gate walk's current index into the positive gap index number.
  Calculate a gate interval given the positive gap index number.
  If the gate inquiry's interval length is less than 42,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  If the gate inquiry's interval length is greater than 963,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Put the gate walk's current position into a prospective positive day.
  Add the gate inquiry's interval length to the prospective positive day.
  Put the prospective positive index number into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the prospective positive day into the gate walk's current position.

\ When moving backwards from a positive gate, its outgoing interval
\ is positiveGap(oldIndex), not a gap at the new smaller index.
\ From zero and below, the interval belongs to the new negative index.
\ Do not publish the new index or coordinate until the gap is valid.
To move to the next negative gate:
  Request another bounded gate step.
  If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
  If the gate walk's current index is less than or equal to -400000000,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Put the gate walk's current index into a prospective negative index number.
  Subtract 1 from the prospective negative index number.
  Put the prospective negative index number into a negative gap index number.
  If the gate walk's current index is greater than 0,
    put the gate walk's current index into the negative gap index number.
  Calculate a gate interval given the negative gap index number.
  If the gate inquiry's interval length is less than 42,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  If the gate inquiry's interval length is greater than 963,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Put the gate walk's current position into a prospective negative day.
  Subtract the gate inquiry's interval length from the prospective negative day.
  Put the prospective negative index number into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the prospective negative day into the gate walk's current position.

\ The signed index must fit CAL's native number type.
\ Each step invokes the exact canonical sauce for its own day pair.
To locate a gate given a number:
  Begin bounded gate walking.
  Put the number into the gate walk's requested index.
  Put 0 into the gate walk's current index.
  Put -15055671 into the gate walk's current position.
  \ An index farther than the remaining step allowance cannot be
  \ reached. Reject it immediately, before any expensive sauce.
  If the number is greater than 1024,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  If the number is less than -1024,
    put 1 into the gate step budget's exhausted; exit.
  Loop.
    If the gate walk's current index is the gate walk's requested index, break.
    If the gate walk's requested index is greater than 0, move to the next positive gate.
    If the gate walk's requested index is less than 0, move to the next negative gate.
    If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
  Repeat.
